pr.body:
With the GitHub sync gone, nothing alerts you when fetching breaks, for example if the CurseForge key stops working. Portainer shows the fetcher as unhealthy, but only if you look. With this change, the fetcher reports each cycle to a Healthchecks.io check, which emails you when fetching keeps failing.
How it behaves
The check is set to a 10-minute period with a 30-minute grace.
- HEALTHCHECKS_PING_URL unset: no reporting, so this is safe to merge before the URL is added.
- Reporting can't affect fetching: it runs after the data and state are saved, with a 10-second timeout. Errors are only logged, and the URL itself is never logged.
- hc-ping.com answers an unknown check with HTTP 200 and OK (not found), so a typo in the UUID gets a clear warning in docker logs instead of looking fine.
- The URL is a plain, optional compose variable rather than a secret, so the fetcher still starts while it's unset. It stays in the server's .env, not in this public repo.
Verification
- A local harness ran 13 scenarios through run_cycle()/report() with recorded pings, all passing:
- no URL, or a non-https URL;
- good cycles (logged only on change);
- one failure while the data is fresh (no ping);
- stale stats or a stale activity feed (/fail with reasons);
- recovery;
- Healthchecks.io unreachable or returning 404 (fetching unaffected);
- an unknown check (warns once), then a fixed URL.
